1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,480
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome back to our head and neck anatomy series.

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:11,480
 In this video, we're going to be talking about the muscles of mastication.

3
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:16,480
 So the muscles of mastication are muscles involved with moving the mandible, providing

4
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:19,440
 the ability to chew or masticate.

5
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:24,280
 They're formed from the paraxial mesoderm of the first pharyngeal arch.

6
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:31,200
 This is that mesodermal tissue that develops on either side of the neural tube in the embryo.

7
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:35,920
 As we talked about in our second video, it's also responsible for forming the posterior

8
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:37,320
 skull.

9
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:41,720
 Since it's coming from the first pharyngeal arch, it's no surprise that these muscles

10
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:48,720
 are being innervated by the trigeminal nerve, cranial nerve 5, specifically the third division

11
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:51,240
 of cranial nerve 5.

12
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:56,760
 All of the muscles of mastication originate from the skull in a place other than the

13
00:00:56,760 --> 00:01:01,800
 mandible, and they insert into the mandible to allow it to move.

14
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:08,760
 So we'll go from outside to inside and start with the masseter muscle.

15
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:14,880
 Now for every muscle that we review, in this video and in the videos to come, we'll recite

16
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:20,760
 the origin, insertion and action for every muscle.

17
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:25,080
 Intervation is the other high yield thing to know for every muscle, but I'll usually

18
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:30,960
 point that out at the beginning of a muscle group, and oftentimes each muscle in that

19
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:37,960
 group is innervated by the same nerve, which makes things a whole lot easier to remember.

20
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:42,880
 So the masseter is actually broken into two distinct parts, a superficial part that we

21
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:49,320
 can clearly see, and a deep part that's peeking out behind the superficial part.

22
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:56,380
 So the superficial part has oblique or diagonally running fibers, and they originate at the anterior

23
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:03,200
 two thirds of the zygomatic arch, and insert into the lateral surface of the ramus down

24
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:04,920
 to the angle.

25
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:10,080
 The deep part has vertical running fibers, it also originates from the zygomatic arch

26
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:16,360
 but a bit more posteriorly, and it also inserts into the lateral surface of the ramus as well

27
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:18,480
 as the angle.

28
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:24,960
 I always think of a muscle like a rope, and the origin is where a person who is standing

29
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:30,760
 still is pulling the rope towards them, and the insertion is whatever on the other end

30
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:33,840
 of the rope getting pulled toward that person.

31
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:40,320
 So the origin is immobile, and the insertion is mobile, and the muscle is what's controlling

32
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:41,920
 that movement.

33
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:44,400
 So what is the masseter's action or function?

34
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:51,680
 Well, it elevates or closes the mandible, and this makes perfect sense because the primary

35
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:58,840
 direction of those fibers is running up towards the zygomatic arch, pulling the insertion toward

36
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:00,480
 the origin.

37
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:07,080
 Secondarily, the superficial fibers can actually protrude the mandible forward, while the deep

38
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:12,480
 fibers can retract or retrude the mandible after it's been protruded.

39
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:19,280
 Again, this makes sense just looking at the orientation of those muscle fibers.

40
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:26,600
 Lastly, if a masseter muscle is activated, only on one side, it can move the mandible

41
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:31,840
 toward that side, so if the left masseter activates, it can move the mandible to the

42
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:32,840
 left.

43
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:36,680
 I'm going to show you something a little bit later towards the end of the video to help

44
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:38,540
 you remember this.

45
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:45,600
 Another way to say this is that unilateral action produces ipsilateral excursion.

46
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:51,560
 That's a lateral movement toward the same side.

47
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:58,440
 So if we cut away the masseter and the zygomatic arch, we see the full temporalis muscle.

48
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:03,960
 The temporalis muscle originates in the temporal fossa, which we spent a whole lot of time talking

49
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:09,320
 about in our fossa video, specifically along the temporal line.

50
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:16,360
 It inserts into the coronoid process of the mandible, and it functions to elevate or close

51
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:18,760
 the mandible, which again makes sense.

52
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:25,600
 If we just look at the orientation of those fibers, the pull is going to be parallel to

53
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:27,240
 those fibers.

54
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:33,880
 Again, the origin is located above the insertion point, so it will pull up on the mandible

55
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:36,160
 at its coronoid process.

56
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:43,120
 Secondarily, the posterior fibers, which run a bit more obliquely, can retrude or retract

57
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:46,520
 the mandible back.

58
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:53,080
 If we cut away the ramus and go even further in toward the infratemporal fossa area, we

59
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:57,440
 see the medial pterogoid muscle down here.

60
00:04:57,440 --> 00:05:02,640
 This one also has a superficial and a deep part, just like the masseter did.

61
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:09,560
 The superficial part here originates at the tuberosity of the maxilla, and also the pyramidal

62
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:12,360
 process of the palatine bone.

63
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:18,880
 The deep part originates from the medial surface of the lateral pterogoid plate, and this is

64
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:24,440
 how it gets its name, not from it being the lateral pterogoid plate, but that it's originating

65
00:05:24,440 --> 00:05:29,680
 from the medial surface of the lateral pterogoid plate.

66
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:36,520
 It inserts into the medial or internal surface of the ramus down to the angle, basically

67
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:40,880
 on the opposite side that the masseter attaches to it.

68
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:45,480
 It elevates the mandible and provides some protrusion.

69
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:50,120
 Again, just look at the direction of the pull of the muscle fibers.

70
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:56,360
 Now, if a medial pterogoid muscle is activated only on one side, it moves the mandible toward

71
00:05:56,360 --> 00:05:58,200
 the opposite side.

72
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:04,540
 So if this left medial pterogoid is activated, the mandible will move to the right.

73
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:13,120
 In other words, unilateral action produces contralateral excursion.

74
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:17,360
 Last but not least, we have the lateral pterogoid muscle.

75
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:21,440
 And this one has a superior and inferior head.

76
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:28,240
 The superior head originates at the infrared temporal crest, and that's the superior border

77
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:35,400
 of the infrared temporal fossa, and it inserts into the articular disc and joint capsule

78
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:38,880
 of the temporal mandibular joint.

79
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:44,720
 The inferior head originates lower down at the lateral surface of the lateral pterogoid

80
00:06:44,720 --> 00:06:51,720
 plate, hence the name lateral pterogoid muscle, and it inserts into the condylar neck at

81
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:57,200
 a depression known as the pterogoid fovea.

82
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:05,240
 Now both superior and inferior head work together to pull the disc and the condyle forward and

83
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:14,440
 down the articular eminence, and this initiates jaw opening and protrudes the mandible.

84
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:24,940
 As with the medial pterogoid muscle, unilateral action produces contralateral excursion.

85
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:30,840
 Now I'm bringing back this image that I showed you in the last video on facial spaces because

86
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:32,680
 honestly this gets really cool.

87
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:38,200
 So this is a cross-section view, technically from the front of the skull looking at the

88
00:07:38,200 --> 00:07:40,240
 right side of the head.

89
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:46,280
 We have the zygomatic arch out here, the mandible, with the coronoid process cut through and

90
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:48,520
 the condyle sitting behind it.

91
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:55,880
 We have the temporal bone appear, the infra temporal crest over here, and the lateral pterogoid

92
00:07:55,880 --> 00:07:59,240
 plate right here.

93
00:07:59,240 --> 00:08:05,200
 Now all four muscles that we just talked about with all of their origins and insertions

94
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:08,680
 are shown in this image on the left.

95
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:15,640
 And those muscles make up those four masticator spaces we discussed in the last video.

96
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:20,400
 And then I showed you this simplified drawing to make a diamond to help you remember how

97
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:25,260
 all those muscles are positioned, and all those bones are positioned to make up those

98
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:27,040
 four quadrants.

99
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:33,240
 But we can also use this drawing for another purpose.

100
00:08:33,240 --> 00:08:37,320
 So all we have to do is highlight the mandible.

101
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:42,600
 And I told you before, I have a really easy way to remember the excursion direction of

102
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:44,400
 each of these muscles.

103
00:08:44,400 --> 00:08:50,240
 So check this out, the temporalis is going to pull on that mandible straight up.

104
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:52,880
 So there's no excursion there.

105
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:56,880
 The masseter pulls on the mandible outward.

106
00:08:56,880 --> 00:09:02,160
 So it's going to pull the mandible toward that same side.

107
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:08,120
 Both pterogoid muscles pull in on the mandible.

108
00:09:08,120 --> 00:09:12,800
 So they're going to want to pull it toward the opposite side.

109
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:19,880
 Now you can also see how the temporalis, the masseter, and the medial pterogoid all pull

110
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:27,000
 up on the mandible, effectively closing it, while the lateral pterogoid pulls the mandible

111
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:33,680
 straight so it moves forward, and as a result of that, we'll also move down along the natural

112
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:36,580
 slope of the articular eminence.

113
00:09:36,580 --> 00:09:40,560
 So that's pretty darn cool.

114
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:42,120
 So that's it for this video guys.

115
00:09:42,120 --> 00:09:43,800
 Thank you so much for watching.

116
